 | Two-parent copying: a child takes each gene from one of two alike parents (15 Oct 2026, the owner agreed on 14 Oct; queued item 3 of 13.2) | **Why.** Faster adaptation: with one parent a gene changes only by the generation's small noise, so a good variant spreads only by its own line; with two, the variants a population holds are recombined in every birth, and selection finds good combinations without waiting for each to arise in one line. And kinds become the groups that mix: likeness already decides who joins a node, so it decides who mixes, and two kinds that are not alike never do. **The law, inside Copy, no sixth law and no new number** (`copy::partner`, `mix`, `mix_rows`). (1) *A partner:* when a mature body makes children (`copy::copy`, which `emit` finishes) it looks in its own cell for another mature body (`Body::adult`; an individual or a node) that is **alike** to it by the likeness that lets bodies join: `copy::alike` on the means with `Physics::alike` (every gene within 4 steps, the sizes of brain and senses by whole units, so a brain 3 units bigger is alike and 6 is not). The most alike one (the least squared gap in steps), a keyed random pick among equals (a draw keyed by the call and the slot, not by the order of the cell's list, which a snapshot does not keep); never itself. It looks in the cell only (the cell is the only reach, no number). All the children of one call have the one partner. (2) *Genes:* each gene of the children is one parent's, a draw of its own for each gene (a keyed draw, `Purpose::Mutate` of the maker), then the generation's mutation noise as before (`vary`). The chance of the partner's is its **share of the members of both** (`n_partner / (n_maker + n_partner)`: a half for two individuals or two nodes of as many members). A group of many children (a node's, or an individual's several) takes about that share of its genes from each (a binomial, drawn exactly for a few and as a normal or a Poisson for many, so the rarer parent gets its due), its mean between the two, and its **spread is the pool of a merge** (`take_in`): each parent's spread by its share, plus the squared gap of the means times the share of each (a quarter of it at halves) per gene; one child gets one parent's gene and that parent's spread. (3) *Born brain rows:* each row (a row is one reader of the hidden layer, the weights of a row go together; a weight drawn alone would break the readout) from one parent by the same draw, the partner's rows carried over to the maker's brain sizes (`brain::rerow`) if they differ; the row spread pooled the same way; the child's lineage and group are the maker's. With a `null` run rows are the maker's. (4) *No partner:* the children are copies of one parent exactly as before, so a lone body still breeds. (5) *Costs:* none. The partner pays nothing and loses nothing: its genes are information, no matter or energy moves (a test holds the totals and the partner's stores, heat, genes, brood and count). (6) *Nodes:* this replaces nothing in the node machinery; nodes still pool members by likeness (`join`, the monthly merge, `within`, the split) and the size and maturity classes are as before; a node can be a partner and have one. `Config::two_parents` (switch, default on, `ew run --no-mix`) puts one parent back: it exists for the check that nodes keep the mean of their genes without selection and to measure what mixing changes. (7) *Tape:* `root_<k>_mixed`, the share of the year's births of the kind with two parents (the birth event carries `mixed`). **Why the partner's chance is its share of the members, found by the test of neutral nodes.** With a half for every partner, the large offspring of a node of a million members took half its genes from a partner of ten, and the mean over all members, which no selection should move, wandered 1.9 steps in 1,000 updates (a limit of 1; one parent 0.2); with the share it is mean-preserving in pairs that choose each other (the sum over births of n x p x (x_partner - x_maker) is zero for a mutual pair). Partners are chosen by the maker alone, so a body whose nearest is another's nearest pulls toward it and the mean still wanders (1.5 steps in the same test): that test now runs with `two_parents` off (the node math alone), and the doubt below. **Found on the way (a bug of snapshots, not of mixing):** a world loaded from a snapshot went on differently from the one it was saved from on some seeds (2 and 4 of 32x32 at 17 months, with or without mixing), because the numbers that follow from a body's genes (`Body::k`) were not saved and others read them (`Body::burns`, `pace`, `visible`) before the body updated; the tape test passed on seed 1 by luck of the trajectory until mixing moved it. `k` is saved with the body now (old snapshots do not load). **Results (64x64, 60 years, mean of years 56 to 60, seeds 1 / 2 / 3, main 8074ad6 in brackets; runs diverge, so only large or all-seed differences count; same seeds are bit-exact repeatable).** Share of births with two parents: plants 12 / 10 / 17%, eaters 7.5 / 10 / 6.8%. Plants (kind 0): members 3.5e10 / 1.3e10 / 1.9e10 (1.3e10 / 1.2e10 / 1.7e10), real life 0.36 / 0.39 / 0.61 y (0.53 / 0.45 / 0.62), life of the adult 0.53 / 0.57 / 0.70 y (0.71 / 0.64 / 1.00), body 0.29 / 0.70 / 0.84 kg (0.80 / 0.67 / 0.82) at a copy mass of 0.42 / 1.00 / 1.48 kg (1.15 / 0.96 / 1.04). Eaters (kind 1): members 6.2e6 / 1.6e7 / 1.6e7 (5.8e6 / 8.3e6 / 2.4e7), real life 1.36 / 0.83 / 1.35 y (1.05 / 0.96 / 0.80), life of the adult 2.5 / 1.5 / 1.7 y (2.7 / 2.4 / 1.9), body 26.9 / 22.7 / 25.7 kg (25.9 / 23.4 / 16.3) at a copy mass of 48 / 44 / 44 kg (41 / 41 / 34). **Did evolution move faster?** The gene means of ALLOC, CHILD, COPY_MASS, REPAIR and HIDDEN, from the kind's first year to years 56 to 60, summed as the move in mutation steps (the step of each gene): plants 4.3 / 0.6 / 3.6 steps (1.1 / 0.5 / 1.5), eaters 0.6 / 0.7 / 0.4 (0.6 / 0.5 / 1.0). Plants moved more on two seeds of three (seed 1: the copy-mass gene fell from 0.07 to -0.40, a plant of 0.4 kg, against 0.09 to 0.06; seed 3: CHILD -0.51 to -0.37 against -0.49 to -0.45, ALLOC 0.82 to 0.85 against 0.84); the eaters not at all. In the roster (kinds 0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7): 3.1 / 0.6 / 8.0 / 0.3 / 7.0 / 1.9 steps (0.8 / 0.5 / 7.8 / 0.6 / 0.9 / 4.5): more in the plants of kinds 0 and 6, the same or less in the rest. So a faster move shows in plants (whose nodes share a cell with many alike mature neighbours), not in the animals, and a run of each is too few to say more. Carbon gas 122 / 174 / 86 ppm (99 / 198 / 166), roster 149 (155). Bodies 2,235 / 2,862 / 3,940 (1,991 / 2,561 / 2,712), roster 6,296 (6,554). Seconds a year 0.76 / 0.99 / 1.09 (0.77 / 0.83 / 0.86), seed 3 run beside its main run alone 1.14 (0.88): 0 to 30% slower, in bodies (more variety: the spread of mixed children is wider, so nodes split more, 4 to 26 and 40,000 splits a year on seeds 1 and 3, and more bodies). **Roster, seed 1:** kinds alive 7 of 10 (6): the same six (0, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7) and kind 8 (16 members of 46 kg at a copy mass of 96 kg; none on main); kinds 2, 3 and 9 are gone in both. Mixed births 4.5 / 1.5 / 5.3 / 9.5 / 4.3 / 6.1% for those kinds. 1.86 s a year (2.23, not run beside each other). No tuning to rescue kinds. **Tests:** new `core/tests/mix.rs` (each gene and born row of a child is one parent's and a half from each, 4,400 genes: 50.0%; no partner, a partner that is not mature, or itself: one parent; unlike bodies never mix: a gene past the limit, a brain 6 units apart, and of two partners the nearer; the partner loses nothing and the totals hold; a node's mixed children have the pooled spread and the mean its partner's share sets, at a half and at a tenth), the tape test reads `root_<k>_mixed`, `core/tests/nodes.rs` `genes_do_not_drift_without_selection` runs with `two_parents` off. **Doubts.** (a) The words "half the squared gap" are taken as a merge pools (a(1-a) times the squared gap, a quarter at halves). (b) The partner is chosen by the maker alone, so the mean over members can wander (above); only mutual pairs keep it. (c) Only 4 to 17% of births are mixed: the partner must be mature, alike in all hundred genes and in the cell, and most nodes in a cell are young. (d) Mixed children's wider spread lets them join nodes a little past `alike` (`merged_wide` was 0 to 1 members a year, now 10^7 on seed 1 and 10^9 on seed 3, of 10^10) and makes more splits and bodies. (e) A child's lineage (brain base) is the maker's even when its rows come from the partner. |
+| A soil nutrient: body matter holds nitrogen and is built of a mineral the ground holds (15 Oct 2026, the owner agreed on 14 Oct; queued item 4 of 13.2) | **Why.** Nothing limited plants but light, liquid and warmth, no place was richer than another, and dead matter that lasts twenty years locked carbon (review 8) but nothing a plant could lack. A mineral that matter is built of, that rot gives back and liquid carries off, gives richer and poorer places, a limit on production, a cycle with a leak, and, with one reaction, a reason to fix the air's nitrogen. **The chemistry** (6.0, `worldgen/src/chem0.toml`, `docs/numbers.md`). Body matter (22) is C40H66N2O34, 1,118 g/mol, hf -6,271 kJ/mol; carbon to nitrogen 20 (tissue is 20 to 50 in plants, about 6 in animals; one matter serves both, so the plants' nitrogen-rich end; 40 and 2 is the smallest unit a salt of two nitrogens fills). Growth (`Chemistry::build`): 40 of the energy-rich solid (5) + 1 of the nitrogen mineral (23) -> 1 of matter + 9 of liquid, thermoneutral: hf(22) = 40 x -212 + -365 - 9 x -286 = -6,271, so building costs `c_copy` as before. The mineral (23) is N2H4O3, 80 g/mol, hf -365, a solid melting at 443 K that dissolves to a mole fraction of 0.3 (190 g in 100 g of the liquid). Burning matter (21): C40H66N2O34 + 40 O2 -> 40 CO2 + 31 H2O + N2H4O3, 18,700 kJ/mol = 16.7 MJ/kg (17.3 before; cellulose 17.5); its rate in the open is the same 5% a year and its `k` the same 5.7e8, because the new `Reaction::ord` gives the rate law the 6th power of the oxygen-like gas the 6-unit matter had (6.2). Fixing (22): N2 + 2 H2O + CO2 -> N2H4O3 + CO, **dH = -365 + -110.5 - (2 x -286 + -393.5) = +490 kJ per mol of the gas = 16 x 30.5**, the 16 ATP of nitrogenase at the standard free energy of the hydrolysis of ATP (488); the reduced carbon gas that leaves carries the energy, as the hydrogen gas nitrogenase gives off carries a part of what it spends. Its barrier, Ea 6,600 K with k 8e-8, is 1e-17 /s at 290 K (3e-10 a year in the open), 1e-10 at 1000 K and 1e-9 at 1500 K; a body needs a speed-up of 10^9 for the 5e-9 /s a growing plant needs; no starting kind has the gene (it is off). Breaking down hot (23): 2 N2H4O3 -> 2 N2 + 4 H2O + O2, -414 kJ, Ea 20,000 K: where matter burns hot the mineral it leaves breaks down and the nitrogen goes to the air (at 1000 K 1,000 mol of matter gave 1,000 mol of the gas and no mineral in the test; at 290 K the mineral returns one for one). **Placed:** 0.357 mol of 23 to the m² of every land cell not under more than half a metre of liquid, 10 g of nitrogen to the m² (mineral nitrogen of a soil is 1 to 10; the top, since the start has no humus), 28.6 g of the salt, 1.75 x 10^8 kg on 1,532 cells of the 64 x 64 world (seed 1), 2.19 x 10^9 mol, booked as new matter and energy like an edit, **when life starts (year 2), not at year 0**: in the first two years of a world, before any body, rain carries a freshly placed mineral from the slopes into the basins and the sea (seed 1, placed at year 0: 46% of the land's mineral had left the land's ground within the first year, 2.24 x 10^9 mol to 1.21; 58% before the soil's moisture was counted), and no body would find it. **In the laws.** (1) `copy::grow` needs the mineral in the formula's ratio: no mineral, no growth; `Diag::nlim` tallies the members that could build and the mineral held back. (2) Flow (6.1, `flow::liquid`): a solid with a solubility that melts above the warm ground dissolves in the liquid of a node up to it and moves with it, in the share of the liquid that runs off of the standing liquid and the soil's moisture together (`Physics::pore`, 0.25 m, new, anchored: the field capacity of the 1 m loose layer); it is not sediment and does not creep; Flow carried no dissolved solid before. The solubilities of plant matter (0.1) and rock (0.001) were read by nothing and are 0. (3) Roots (`body::live`): a body with the uptake gene on for such a solid takes it from the ground of its cell up to the level its 