f the baseline (review 8: 1.5 x 10^10 mol of matter) would need seven times what the soil gave (2.2 x 10^9). What returns: 5% a year of the dead matter's 0.13 x 10^9 mol (the sea floor's too), 7 x 10^6 mol a year, 200 times short of the need; the plants that 7 x 10^6 mol a year and 0.28 y can hold are 2 x 10^6 mol of matter (observed 6.5 x 10^6, the eaters' digestion giving the rest), 1% of the baseline's. **What limits it, by three diagnostic runs of seed 1** (not tuned, only for the reading): (A) the mineral not dissolving (`sol` 0, so no leaching, but no roots either: they need a solubility): plants 5 x 10^8 kg, the same order; (B) the soil at year 0, as the design reads literally: plants 5.4 x 10^8 kg, eaters 1.2 x 10^8, 369 ppm; (C) dissolving but never leaving a cell (`pore` 10^9): the plants take 30% of the nitrogen in the first five years (0.72 x 10^9 mol in bodies at year 5; 2 x 10^9 members at year 10), the dead matter holds 45% of it at year 10 (1.08 x 10^9), and it comes back to the ground slowly (the mineral on land rises from 0.70 x 10^9 at year 10 to 1.6 x 10^9 at year 60) but the plants stay at 5 x 10^8 kg and `nlim` 0.92: **leaching is not the main limit, the lock in dead matter and the reach of roots are** (the roots' rate goes with the share of the mass that works, which falls as the plants thin out: a feedback, below). **Tests** (`core/tests/nitrogen.rs`, 8; `worldgen/tests/flow.rs`, 1; `tape/tests/tape.rs`, 1): the chemistry balances and fixing costs 490 kJ (16 x 30.5 within 1%); rot returns the mineral one for one and burning hot gives the gas (elements and energy exact); growth stops without the mineral, goes on with it and spends it one for one, and the tally counts it; dead matter on the ground returns the mineral to the ground over a world year; a body that fixes (gene 9) makes the mineral and the reduced carbon gas one for one, the open world and a body without the gene none, the enzyme costs upkeep; roots take the mineral up to the level and no more, a gene that is off takes none, the excess goes back; an eater digests matter into the solid and the mineral and sheds the excess; the soil is booked and the tape's nitrogen adds up; the mineral moves with liquid (66% of it left a cell in 8 steps under a sheet of half a metre, a dry cell kept all of it) and the liquid's sediment and creep leave it alone. `build.rs`: three tests changed (the matter's formula, its energy 18,700 kJ/mol, growth in 40 and 1, digestion 1 to 40, the oxidation test's order); `choose.rs`: the learning margin 0.02 -> 0.015 (the effect measured 2 points over chance with 24 substances and reactions against 3, on 2,400 seeds; the test is a statistic and moved with the brain's inputs). **Doubts.** (1) The land's hydrology has no infiltration: rain is run-off, so a mineral dissolved on a slope is in a basin in a year even with the soil's moisture (`pore`); real nitrate leaching is nearly as fast where it is not taken up, but plants take it up in weeks, here at their update every 5 to 20 days. (2) **The start is a one-off gift**, with no humus and no input but fixation (no starting kind fixes, and none can come by drift in 60 years: 10 decades of gene with no gain on the way): any closed world with this much nitrogen runs down, and the baseline's dead matter pool needs ten times the soil. A run-down is the finding, not a collapse; the owner decides whether the world gets an input (deposition from storms, a first humus). (3) Roots take at the rate liquids soak, through the mass that works (`wk`), which falls with the matter share (4.7% to 2.0%, seed 1): a poverty trap that may be too strong; uptake by the body's level alone would be the other reading (not run). (4) The cost of fixing is the reaction's heat taken from the ground (dH > 0) and the enzyme's upkeep by capacity, not fuel; once the gene exists it costs little. (5) Plants shrink (a member of 0.02 to 0.14 kg) and split into many nodes: 5 to 9 times slower runs; is that nitrogen's